{"name":"Night Sky","hex":"#292b31","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"night sky","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The sky as it is seen at night."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/night_sky"}],"entries":[{"title":"Night Sky (TV series)","blocks":[{"text":"Night Sky is an American science fiction drama television series created by Holden Miller for Amazon Studios and Legendary Television. It stars Sissy Spacek and J. K. Simmons as a couple who possess a chamber leading to another planet. Chai Hansen, Adam Bartley, Julieta Zylberberg, Rocío Hernández, Kiah McKirnan, Beth Lacke, Stephen Louis Grush, and Cass Buggé co-star. The series was shot in Illinois and Jujuy Province, Argentina."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/e/ec/Night_Sky_%28TV_series%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Night_Sky_(TV_series)","wikidata":"Q107144760"},{"title":"Night Skies","blocks":[{"text":"Night Skies is an unproduced science fiction horror film that was in development following the success of Steven Spielberg's Close Encounters of the Third Kind (1977). Although Night Skies was never made, some of its ideas inspired E.T. the Extra-Terrestrial (1982), Poltergeist (1982), and Gremlins (1984)."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Night_Skies","wikidata":"Q7033459"},{"title":"Night sky","blocks":[{"text":"The night sky is the nighttime appearance of celestial objects like stars, planets, and the Moon, which are visible in a clear sky between sunset and sunrise, when the Sun is below the horizon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/%D0%A1%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%82_%D0%BE%D1%82_%D0%B4%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%BD%D0%B8_-_panoramio.jpg/330px-%D0%A1%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%82_%D0%BE%D1%82_%D0%B4%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%BD%D0%B8_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Night_sky","wikidata":"Q1153471"},{"title":"Night","blocks":[{"text":"Night, or nighttime, is the period of darkness when the Sun is below the horizon. Daylight illuminates one side of Earth, leaving the other in darkness. The opposite of nighttime is daytime. Earth's rotation causes the appearance of sunrise and sunset. Moonlight, airglow, starlight, and light pollution dimly illuminate night. The duration of day, night, and twilight varies depending on the time of year and the observer's latitude."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Savault_Chapel_Under_Milky_Way_BLS.jpg/330px-Savault_Chapel_Under_Milky_Way_BLS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Night","wikidata":"Q575"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Starry_Night_at_La_Silla.jpg/500px-Starry_Night_at_La_Silla.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Starry_Night_at_La_Silla.jpg/1280px-Starry_Night_at_La_Silla.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Starry_Night_at_La_Silla.jpg/500px-Starry_Night_at_La_Silla.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Milky Way is brighter in the Southern Hemisphere than in the North. (Photo taken at La Silla Observatory)[2] ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStarry_Night_at_La_Silla.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/%D0%9F%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BB%D1%8C%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%87%D0%B8.jpg/500px-%D0%9F%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BB%D1%8C%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%87%D0%B8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wik